# engineering.rules -- preserved governance content as Starlark comments.
# This file contains no executable `prefix_rule` entries because its guidance is
# not command-execution policy.
#
# ---
# trigger: always_on
# ---
# 
# # Engineering
# 
# ## Workflow
# - Understand the problem, success criteria, and verification plan before editing code.
# - Explore the codebase: identify entry points, data flows, invariants, and existing tests relevant to the change.
# - Use the project’s configured environment (venv/conda/poetry/etc.) and keep dependencies pinned.
# - Prefer extending existing patterns over introducing new frameworks or styles.
# - Keep temporary artifacts (notes, scratch scripts) local; delete or clearly archive when no longer needed.
# 
# ## Coding
# - Design first: data shapes, interfaces, failure modes, and performance expectations.
# - Write small, composable functions with clear responsibilities and minimal side effects.
# - Optimize for readability and maintainability over cleverness; follow the local style.
# - Keep functions and modules focused; split or rename when responsibilities drift.
# - Use explicit names for variables, functions, and modules that reflect intent, not implementation details.
# - Fail fast with informative errors; include enough context to debug without reproducing.
# - Log at appropriate levels (debug/info/warn/error) and avoid noisy or personally identifiable data.
# - Keep dependencies minimal; prefer standard library and existing project utilities.
# - Apply linters/formatters incrementally; if they fight the code, refine the code or configuration, not the rules.
# - Prefer testable units over hard-to-mock global state or singletons.
# 
# ## Change Impact
# - Never evaluate a change in isolation: trace callers, callees, data contracts, shared state, and end-to-end flows.
# - Preserve and respect existing invariants, assumptions, and public contracts; document any intentional breaks.
# - Consider concurrency, retries, timeouts, and failure recovery for I/O and long-running operations.
# - Assess security, privacy, and performance implications for every externally visible change.
# - Prefer backwards-compatible changes; if breaking behavior, document migration and rationale clearly.
# 
# 
# ## Collaboration & Review
# - Keep changesets small, coherent, and focused on a single purpose.
# - Explain “why” in commit messages and PR descriptions; the diff already shows “what”.
# - Prefer clarity in code over lengthy comments; comment only where intent or tradeoffs are non-obvious.
# - Respond to review feedback constructively; resolve disagreements with data, references, or small experiments.
# - Leave the codebase slightly better than you found it: clarify names, reduce duplication, and update docs where needed.
